#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087453 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbf50e27-a8c3-52e7-be21-5d7ed61e5512" :knowledge-point/title "Filling a Table of Values for a Parametric Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "53ce2176-ae44-578d-8fba-35c5018d4162" :question/math-academy-id "e-5075" :question/problem "For the parametric curve\n$x=3t, \\,\\,\\, y=t^3, \\quad -3\\leq t \\leq 3$,\n\nfill in the following table of values.\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=3t$. This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | $-9$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we work out the $y$-values using $y=t^3$. This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | $-9$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| $y$ | $-27$ | $-8$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $8$ | $27$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087457 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0dc11d5-8eda-52b1-b7b1-42b481813ff4" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Curve Defined Parametrically"}] :question/id #uuid "b322617c-ab21-5ce8-997f-2f10813b4280" :question/math-academy-id "e-5098" :question/problem "Plot the graph of the parametrically defined curve\n$x=t(t-6), \\,\\,\\, y=t^3, \\quad -2\\leq t \\leq 2$." :question/worked-solution "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=t(t-6)$. This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | $16$ | $7$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-8$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=t^3$. This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | $16$ | $7$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-8$ |\n| $y$ | $-8$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $8$ |\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/803/Source/Images/e492a940fa95b406b60c6b0a5c438d98.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087458 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cbf0ad0-a7c5-5c0f-a06c-9210fdc244ef" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Range of a Parametric Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "a792fa16-e1fd-5f30-a128-bbc49a285429" :question/math-academy-id "e-5099" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n$x=t^2-6t, \\,\\,\\, y=t^4+4, \\quad t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$,\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y$?" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x$, we can start by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}x & = {t}^{2} - 6 t \\\\  & = {( t - 3 )}^{2} - ( 3 {)}^{2} \\\\  & = {( t - 3 )}^{2} - 9\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSince $\\left(t-3\\right)^2 \\geq 0$, we can conclude that $x \\geq -9$.\n- To find the range of $y=t^4+4$, notice that $t^4 \\geq 0$, and consequently $y \\geq 4$.\n\nTherefore, the ranges are\n\n$$\nx \\geq -9\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny \\geq 4\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087477 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cddc940d-2185-569c-a62b-b507e95d3c35" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Range of a Parametric Equation Defined in Terms of Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "25ab094c-a35e-5507-a6df-459361ff6fcd" :question/math-academy-id "e-5292" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n$x = 4\\cos\\theta, \\,\\,\\, y = 2\\sin\\theta, \\quad 0\\leq \\theta < 2\\pi$,\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y$?" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x$, notice that for $0\\leq\\theta < 2\\pi$, we have $-1 \\leq \\cos{\\theta} \\leq 1$. Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}- 1 & \\le  \\cos   \\theta \\le  1 \\\\ - 4 & \\le  4 \\cos   \\theta \\le  4 \\\\ - 4 & \\le  x \\le  4.\\end{array}\n$$\n\n.\nSo, we can conclude that $-4 \\leq x \\leq 4$.\n- To find the range of $y$, notice that for $0\\leq\\theta < 2\\pi$, we have $-1 \\leq \\sin{\\theta} \\leq 1$. Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}- 1 & \\le  \\sin   \\theta \\le  1 \\\\ - 2 & \\le  2 \\sin   \\theta \\le  2 \\\\ - 2 & \\le  y \\le  2.\\end{array}\n$$\n\n.\nSo, we can conclude that $-2 \\leq y \\leq 2$.\n\nTherefore, the ranges are\n\n$$\n-4 \\leq x \\leq 4\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n-2 \\leq y \\leq 2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263656 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cbf0ad0-a7c5-5c0f-a06c-9210fdc244ef" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Range of a Parametric Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e2e46fa-845d-5d2a-9713-4edf129f2a99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y<-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263658} {:answer/id #uuid "18400921-7a23-5e3d-a8b5-b49660d0f54c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y>1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263659} {:answer/id #uuid "b017aae2-e626-5e99-a5a0-31b4b61e867a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y<1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263660} {:answer/id #uuid "d25af62e-d94f-55c4-a74f-322857e39e8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y>-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263661} {:answer/id #uuid "40192662-5865-5218-9c2e-334a4e93b7ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b017aae2-e626-5e99-a5a0-31b4b61e867a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y<1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263660} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0c409ee-32bd-5c3e-943c-2a7ccfbff6cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263657}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "12184c96-fdfc-58db-88c7-0f863d4011b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-123767" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{t},\\,\\,\\,y=1-t,\\,t>0,\n$$\n\nwhat is the range of $y?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the range of $y,$ note that $y=1-t<1$ for all $t>0.$ \n\nConsequently, the range of $y$ is $y<1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263677 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0dc11d5-8eda-52b1-b7b1-42b481813ff4" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Curve Defined Parametrically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "514a2638-a302-5ed7-8cbf-3aab79f72f98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13948638/assets/bcb2b08bab14ce74347d37a0e896451750db0203ab830b835a83de6b7a81b61d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263679} {:answer/id #uuid "fd28883e-4733-5298-9fbf-a5b94211513d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13948638/assets/d1cec5ea2da4a0b5f141bb24b4addc797defbc48e76cdfdbfab3a303e348b8cc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263680} {:answer/id #uuid "6cf0cc00-f72d-5fc9-9bfe-0e8290eae30a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13948638/assets/02b248ea373f295fa4dbad215eba4c211ef18548dca4a56d57cb3f15424ad501.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263681} {:answer/id #uuid "c3905ece-0f35-5d7e-baa3-9a8e05309f52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13948638/assets/c477137fe47dc3f0475f44658117545da3dae94f18e3bf2ce897d2a89fad2a37.